-- title:
--   Uniform p-power exponent for base change onto η₀
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, a ring homomorphism $\iota\colon W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})\to W(k)$ (here $\mathrm{Zp2}\,p = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$), and a formal $O_D$-module $\Phi$ of dimension $2$ over $W(k)/p$, where $\mathrm{pIdeal}$ denotes the ideal generated by $p$. Assume: $\Phi$ is special for the reduction $\bar\iota$ of $\iota$, i.e. the submodules $\mathrm{lieZero}$ and $\mathrm{lieOne}$ of $\mathrm{Lie}(\Phi)$ cut out by the $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$-action are complementary and invertible; $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the kernel of its action by $p$ has degree $p^4$; $\mathrm{lieZero}(\bar\iota)$ lies in the kernel of the linear part $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$ of $\varpi$; and the two graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ — the $f$ with $\mathrm{endAct}(\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}\,\tau(c))f = \mathrm{homothety}(\bar\iota(\tau(c))^{p^n})f$ for all $c\in\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ — are complementary, giving graded Cartier module data $D_\Phi$ with its $N$-module $N(D_\Phi)$. Fix an additive map $r_\Phi\colon \mathbb{Z}_p^2\to N(D_\Phi)$ which, for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on $D_\Phi$, maps the whole of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto $\mathrm{etaPiece}\,L\,0$, the intersection of $\mathrm{eta}(L)$ with the degree-$0$ part of $N(D_\Phi)$. Fix further an algebraically closed field $K$ of characteristic $p$ which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra with $p$ nilpotent, a ring homomorphism $\psi'\colon W(k)\to K$, and a rigidified object $t'=(X,n,\rho)$ over $K$ that is admissible for $(\iota,\psi')$: $X$ is special for $\psi'\circ\iota$, has height $4$, and $\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4n$ from $\Phi$ base-changed along $\psi'$ to the reduction $\bar X$. All of this is read over the localisation of $K$ away from $1$, where complementarity of the graded pieces is assumed for $X$ ($hc$), for $\bar X$ ($hcb$) and for $\bar\Phi$ ($hc\Phi1$), and a canonical $L$-map $L'$ on the data of $X$ is fixed. The conclusion: for every canonical $L$-map $L_\Phi$ on the graded Cartier module data $D_{\bar\Phi}$ of $\bar\Phi$ there exists a single $N\in\mathbb{N}$ such that every $x\in\mathrm{etaPiece}\,L_\Phi\,0\subseteq N(D_{\bar\Phi})$ admits $w\in\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ with $p^N\cdot x = N(\mathrm{bc})(r_\Phi(w))$, where $N(\mathrm{bc})$ is the map $N(D_\Phi)\to N(D_{\bar\Phi})$ induced by the base-change morphism $\mathrm{bcPhi}$ of Cartier modules along the residue map of $\psi'$ followed by reduction.
--
--   This is the cofinality step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation package: the $\eta_0$-part of the $N$-module of the fibre $\bar\Phi$ is captured, up to one bounded power of $p$ valid for all elements simultaneously, by base change of the rigidification $r_\Phi$ of the base point. It feeds the construction of coordinates on rigidified fibres, being the uniform-exponent form of the cofinality input used there.
